            <!-- Section 1 -->
            <div class="card shadow-sm border-0 rounded-4 mb-4 section-card">
                <div class="card-body p-4">
                    <h3 class="fw-bold mb-3"><span class="section-num">1</span> 📖 What is Docker? (Simple Words)</h3>
                    <p>
                        <strong>Docker</strong> is a tool that puts your app and
                        everything it needs into a <strong>container</strong>.
                    </p>
                    <p>
                        A container is like a small lunchbox. Inside the box:
                    </p>
                    <ul class="styled-list">
                        <li><i class="fas fa-cube text-primary"></i> Your app</li>
                        <li><i class="fas fa-cube text-primary"></i> The right version of .NET</li>
                        <li><i class="fas fa-cube text-primary"></i> All the settings and libraries</li>
                    </ul>
                    <p class="mb-0">
                        Because everything is inside the box, the app works the
                        same on your laptop, on the test server, and in the cloud.
                    </p>

                    <div class="alert alert-primary border-0 bg-primary bg-opacity-10 mt-3">
                        <strong>💡 Think of it like this:</strong><br />
                        Imagine you move to a new house. Instead of packing
                        everything separately, you pack one box with everything
                        you need. Wherever you go, the box works the same.
                        Docker is that box for your app.
                    </div>
                </div>
            </div>

            <!-- Section 2 -->
            <div class="card shadow-sm border-0 rounded-4 mb-4 section-card">
                <div class="card-body p-4">
                    <h3 class="fw-bold mb-3"><span class="section-num">2</span> ⚖️ Container vs Virtual Machine</h3>
                    <p>
                        Many people confuse containers with virtual machines.
                        They are very different.
                    </p>
                    <div class="table-responsive">
                        <table class="table table-bordered table-hover align-middle">
                            <thead class="table-light">
                                <tr>
                                    <th>Feature</th>
                                    <th>Virtual Machine (VM)</th>
                                    <th>Docker Container</th>
                                </tr>
                            </thead>
                            <tbody>
                                <tr><td>Size</td><td>Gigabytes (GB)</td><td>Megabytes (MB)</td></tr>
                                <tr><td>Startup time</td><td>Minutes</td><td>Seconds</td></tr>
                                <tr><td>Operating System</td><td>Full OS inside</td><td>Shares host OS</td></tr>
                                <tr><td>Resource use</td><td>Heavy</td><td>Light</td></tr>
                                <tr><td>Speed</td><td>Slow 🐢</td><td>Fast 🐇</td></tr>
                            </tbody>
                        </table>
                    </div>
                    <p class="mb-0">
                        <strong>Simple rule:</strong> Use Docker for quick and cheap
                        packaging. Use VMs when you need full separation.
                    </p>
                </div>
            </div>

            <!-- JOKE 2 -->
            <div class="joke-card">
                <div class="joke-emoji">😄</div>
                <div class="joke-body">
                    <strong>DevOps Joke #2:</strong>
                    <p class="mb-0">
                        A developer says: "It works on my machine!"
                        <br />
                        A DevOps engineer replies: "Then we'll ship <em>your machine</em> to the customer."
                        <br />
                        <strong>Docker:</strong> "Hold my container." 🐳
                    </p>
