            <!-- Section 7: Dockerfile -->
            <div class="card shadow-sm border-0 rounded-4 mb-4 section-card">
                <div class="card-body p-4">
                    <h3 class="fw-bold mb-3"><span class="section-num">7</span> ✍️ Your First Dockerfile (Line by Line)</h3>
                    <p>A <strong>Dockerfile</strong> is a text file with instructions. Here is one for a .NET app:</p>

                    <pre><code># Use the official .NET SDK image to build the app
FROM mcr.microsoft.com/dotnet/sdk:8.0 AS build
WORKDIR /app

# Copy the project file and restore dependencies
COPY *.csproj ./
RUN dotnet restore

# Copy the rest of the code and build it
COPY . ./
RUN dotnet publish -c Release -o out

# Use a smaller runtime image for the final container
FROM mcr.microsoft.com/dotnet/aspnet:8.0
WORKDIR /app
COPY --from=build /app/out .

# Tell Docker what command to run
ENTRYPOINT ["dotnet", "MyApp.dll"]</code></pre>

                    <h5 class="fw-bold mt-4 mb-3">📘 What Each Line Does</h5>
                    <div class="table-responsive">
                        <table class="table table-bordered align-middle">
                            <thead class="table-light"><tr><th>Line</th><th>What It Does</th></tr></thead>
                            <tbody>
                                <tr><td><code>FROM ... AS build</code></td><td>Starts from the SDK image. Names this stage "build".</td></tr>
                                <tr><td><code>WORKDIR /app</code></td><td>Sets the working folder inside the container.</td></tr>
                                <tr><td><code>COPY *.csproj ./</code></td><td>Copies the project file into the container.</td></tr>
                                <tr><td><code>RUN dotnet restore</code></td><td>Downloads NuGet packages.</td></tr>
                                <tr><td><code>COPY . ./</code></td><td>Copies your source code.</td></tr>
                                <tr><td><code>RUN dotnet publish -c Release -o out</code></td><td>Builds the app in Release mode.</td></tr>
                                <tr><td><code>FROM ...aspnet:8.0</code></td><td>New, smaller image for runtime.</td></tr>
                                <tr><td><code>COPY --from=build /app/out .</code></td><td>Copies only the built files.</td></tr>
                                <tr><td><code>ENTRYPOINT ["dotnet", "MyApp.dll"]</code></td><td>Command to run when the container starts.</td></tr>
                            </tbody>
                        </table>
                    </div>

                    <div class="alert alert-info border-0 bg-info bg-opacity-10 mt-3">
                        <strong>💡 Why two FROM lines?</strong><br />
                        "build" is big (~1 GB). "runtime" is small (~200 MB).
                        This is a <strong>multi-stage build</strong> — smaller, faster, safer.
                    </div>
                </div>
            </div>

            <!-- Section 8: Commands -->
            <div class="card shadow-sm border-0 rounded-4 mb-4 section-card">
                <div class="card-body p-4">
                    <h3 class="fw-bold mb-3"><span class="section-num">8</span> ⌨️ Basic Docker Commands</h3>
                    <div class="table-responsive">
                        <table class="table table-bordered align-middle">
                            <thead class="table-light"><tr><th>Command</th><th>What It Does</th></tr></thead>
                            <tbody>
                                <tr><td><code>docker build -t myapp .</code></td><td>Builds an image named "myapp".</td></tr>
                                <tr><td><code>docker run -p 8080:80 myapp</code></td><td>Runs and maps port 8080 → 80.</td></tr>
                                <tr><td><code>docker ps</code></td><td>Shows running containers.</td></tr>
                                <tr><td><code>docker ps -a</code></td><td>Shows all containers.</td></tr>
                                <tr><td><code>docker stop &lt;id&gt;</code></td><td>Stops a container.</td></tr>
                                <tr><td><code>docker rm &lt;id&gt;</code></td><td>Deletes a stopped container.</td></tr>
                                <tr><td><code>docker images</code></td><td>Lists all images.</td></tr>
                                <tr><td><code>docker rmi &lt;id&gt;</code></td><td>Deletes an image.</td></tr>
                                <tr><td><code>docker logs &lt;id&gt;</code></td><td>Shows logs (great for debugging).</td></tr>
                                <tr><td><code>docker exec -it &lt;id&gt; bash</code></td><td>Opens a terminal inside a container.</td></tr>
                            </tbody>
                        </table>
                    </div>
                </div>
            </div>

            <!-- Section 9: Hands-On -->
            <div class="card shadow-sm border-0 rounded-4 mb-4 border-start border-4 border-success section-card">
                <div class="card-body p-4">
                    <h3 class="fw-bold mb-3"><span class="section-num">9</span> 🛠️ Hands-On Practice (Do This Now)</h3>

                    <div class="step-box"><strong>Step 1: Verify installation</strong>
                        <p class="mb-0 small mt-1">Run <code>docker --version</code>. Expect <code>Docker version 24.0.0</code> or similar.</p>
                    </div>
                    <div class="step-box"><strong>Step 2: Run your first container</strong>
                        <p class="mb-0 small mt-1">Run <code>docker run hello-world</code>. Docker downloads a tiny image and prints a message.</p>
                    </div>
                    <div class="step-box"><strong>Step 3: Run a real web server</strong>
                        <p class="mb-0 small mt-1">Run <code>docker run -d -p 8080:80 nginx</code>. Open <code>http://localhost:8080</code> in a browser. Stop it with <code>docker stop &lt;id&gt;</code>.</p>
                    </div>
                    <div class="step-box"><strong>Step 4: Build your own image</strong>
                        <p class="mb-0 small mt-1">Create a <code>Dockerfile</code> (from Section 7). Put your .NET project next to it. Run <code>docker build -t my-first-app .</code>.</p>
                    </div>
                    <div class="step-box"><strong>Step 5: Run your app</strong>
                        <p class="mb-0 small mt-1">Run <code>docker run -d -p 8080:80 my-first-app</code>. Open the browser. Your app is live!</p>
                    </div>
                </div>
            </div>

            <!-- JOKE 4 -->
            <div class="joke-card">
                <div class="joke-emoji">😆</div>
                <div class="joke-body">
                    <strong>DevOps Joke #4:</strong>
                    <p class="mb-0">
                        How many DevOps engineers does it take to change a light bulb?
