            <!-- Section 10: Common Problems -->
            <div class="card shadow-sm border-0 rounded-4 mb-4 section-card">
                <div class="card-body p-4">
                    <h3 class="fw-bold mb-3"><span class="section-num">10</span> 🔧 Common Problems and Fixes</h3>
                    <div class="table-responsive">
                        <table class="table table-bordered align-middle">
                            <thead class="table-light"><tr><th>Problem</th><th>Cause</th><th>Fix</th></tr></thead>
                            <tbody>
                                <tr><td>Port is in use</td><td>Another app uses it.</td><td>Change the port: <code>-p 8081:80</code></td></tr>
                                <tr><td>Container stops instantly</td><td>App crashed on start.</td><td>Check logs: <code>docker logs &lt;id&gt;</code></td></tr>
                                <tr><td>"docker: command not found"</td><td>Not installed.</td><td>Install Docker Desktop.</td></tr>
                                <tr><td>Build is slow</td><td>Rebuilding too much.</td><td>Add a <code>.dockerignore</code>.</td></tr>
                                <tr><td>Data disappears</td><td>Container data is temporary.</td><td>Use a volume: <code>-v</code></td></tr>
                            </tbody>
                        </table>
                    </div>
                </div>
            </div>

            <!-- Section 11: REAL LIFE BUILD PROJECT -->
            <div class="card shadow-sm border-0 rounded-4 mb-4 real-project-card">
                <div class="card-body p-4">
                    <span class="badge bg-danger mb-2">🎓 Final Project</span>
                    <h3 class="fw-bold mb-3"><span class="section-num">11</span> 🏗️ Build a Real Docker Project (End-to-End)</h3>
                    <p class="lead text-muted">
                        Build, containerize, run, and share a real ASP.NET Core app.
                        Follow this exactly and you will have a working Docker project.
                    </p>

                    <h5 class="fw-bold mt-4 mb-2">🎯 What You Will Build</h5>
                    <p>A simple "Hello World" ASP.NET Core web app, packaged in a Docker container, running on your computer.</p>

                    <h5 class="fw-bold mt-4 mb-2">📁 Project Folder</h5>
                    <p>Create a folder called <code>HelloDocker</code> anywhere on your PC.</p>

                    <h5 class="fw-bold mt-4 mb-2">🛠️ Step 1: Create the App</h5>
                    <p>Open a terminal in that folder and run:</p>
                    <pre><code>dotnet new web -n HelloDocker
cd HelloDocker</code></pre>

                    <h5 class="fw-bold mt-4 mb-2">🛠️ Step 2: Change the Program.cs</h5>
                    <p>Open <code>Program.cs</code>. Replace everything with:</p>
                    <pre><code>var builder = WebApplication.CreateBuilder(args);
var app = builder.Build();

app.MapGet("/", () => "Hello from Docker! 🐳 My first container is alive!");

app.Run();</code></pre>

                    <h5 class="fw-bold mt-4 mb-2">🛠️ Step 3: Add a Dockerfile</h5>
                    <p>In the <code>HelloDocker</code> folder, create a new file called <code>Dockerfile</code> (no extension). Paste:</p>
                    <pre><code>FROM mcr.microsoft.com/dotnet/sdk:8.0 AS build
WORKDIR /app
COPY *.csproj ./
RUN dotnet restore
COPY . ./
RUN dotnet publish -c Release -o out

FROM mcr.microsoft.com/dotnet/aspnet:8.0
WORKDIR /app
COPY --from=build /app/out .
ENTRYPOINT ["dotnet", "HelloDocker.dll"]</code></pre>

                    <h5 class="fw-bold mt-4 mb-2">🛠️ Step 4: Add .dockerignore</h5>
                    <p>Create a file called <code>.dockerignore</code> with:</p>
                    <pre><code>bin/
obj/
*.user
*.suo</code></pre>
                    <p class="text-muted small">This keeps the image small and build fast.</p>

                    <h5 class="fw-bold mt-4 mb-2">🛠️ Step 5: Build the Image</h5>
                    <p>In the same folder, run:</p>
                    <pre><code>docker build -t hello-docker .</code></pre>
                    <p class="text-muted small">Wait for it to finish. The first time may take 1–2 minutes.</p>

                    <h5 class="fw-bold mt-4 mb-2">🛠️ Step 6: Run the Container</h5>
                    <pre><code>docker run -d -p 8080:80 --name my-hello-app hello-docker</code></pre>
                    <p>This runs the container in the background.</p>

                    <h5 class="fw-bold mt-4 mb-2">🛠️ Step 7: Test It</h5>
                    <p>Open your browser and go to:</p>
                    <pre><code>http://localhost:8080</code></pre>
                    <p>You should see: <strong>"Hello from Docker! 🐳 My first container is alive!"</strong></p>

                    <h5 class="fw-bold mt-4 mb-2">🛠️ Step 8: Manage the Container</h5>
                    <pre><code>docker ps                # see it running
docker logs my-hello-app # see logs
docker stop my-hello-app # stop it
docker rm my-hello-app   # delete it</code></pre>

                    <h5 class="fw-bold mt-4 mb-2">🛠️ Step 9: Share It (Bonus)</h5>
